ong-term care for older people: package for universal health coverage†cdn.who.int】\nL86: \nL87: 【67†Download (8.1 MB)†iris.who.int】\nL88: ### Overview\nL89: \nL90: This publication includes a list of LTC interventions that all countries could consider, prioritize and provide and could integrate within health and social care sectors, depending on their context. The package should be implemented in line with overall plans to design, establish and expand formal, integrated LTC systems and services towards universal health coverage (UHC).\nL91: The package is intended primarily for governments and policy-makers responsible for planning and implementing LTC service provision at national or subnational level. It provides guidance in foreseeing and mainstreaming essential LTC interventions, which can be contextualized according to local needs and resources. The package is neither a clinical guideline for LTC service providers in daily practice nor a manual, standard operating procedures or specific steps.\nL92: \nL93: WHO Team\nL94: Global Network on Long-term Care (GNLTC), Sexual, Reproductive, Maternal, Child, Adolescent and Ageing Health (LHR)\nL95: \nL96: Editors\nL97: \nL98: WHO, Decade of Health Ageing\nL99: \nL100: Number of pages\nL101: \nL102: 85\nL103: \nL104: Reference numbers\nL105: \nL106: ISBN: 9789240086555\nL107: \nL108: Copyright\nL109: \nL110: 【68†Newsletters】\nL111: \nL112: 【69†Report misconduct】\nL113: \nL114: 【70†Privacy policy】\nL115: \nL116: © 2026\nL117: \nL118: 【71†WHO】\nL119: \nL120: [Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrint (https://remittanceprices.worldbank.org/sites/default/files/2026-04/RPW_main_report_and_annex_Q325.pdf)\nciteturn2411view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 32; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://remittanceprices.worldbank.org/sites/default/files/2026-04/RPW_main_report_and_annex_Q325.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 940\nL0@P0:  Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 49, March 2024\nL1@P0: 03\nL2@P0: Official Use Only\nL3@P0-1:  Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL4@P1: 2\nL5@P1: Official Use Only\nL6@P1: This Report reflects the latest trends observed\nL7@P1: in the data published in September 2025.\nL8@P1: Remittance Prices Worldwide is available at\nL9@P1: http://remittanceprices.worldbank.org\nL10@P1-2: © The World Bank Group, 2025Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL11@P2: 3\nL12@P2: Official Use Only\nL13@P2: Key findings\nL14@P2: • The Global Average decreased from 6.49 percent in Q1 2025 to 6.36 percent in Q3 2025.\nL15@P2: • The International MTO Index experienced a decrease over the quarter to 5.52 percent, from 5.91\nL16@P2: percent in Q1 2025.\nL17@P2: • The Global SmaRT Average remained the same at 3.29 percent in Q3 2025. Twenty corridors did\nL18@P2: not have any SmaRT qualifying services.\nL19@P2: • The Digital remittances index decreased to 4.59 percent from 4.85 percent in Q1 2025.\nL20@P2: • The Digital-only MTO index experienced a decrease to 3.54 percent from 3.55 percent in Q1 2025.\nL21@P2: • Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan & Pakistan overtook South Asia as the lowest cost receiving\nL22@P2: region, with an average cost of 5.11 percent. Sub-Saharan Africa remains the most expensive region\nL23@P2: to send money to, recorded at 8.46 percent total average cost.\nL24@P2: • Banks remain the most expensive type of service provider, with an average cost of 14.99 percent.\nL25@P2: • The proportion of corridors with average costs of less than 5 percent has increased considerably\nL26@P2: since Q1 2009 (from 17 percent to 83 percent in Q3 2025).\nL27@P2: • Credit/debit card became the lowest cost instrument to originate remittances, averaging 4.39 percent,\nL28@P2: while debit card became the lowest cost instrument to receive remittances, at 3.61 percent.\nL29@P2: • Due to the ongoing Russia-Ukraine war, RPW did not include any data on corridors originating in the\nL30@P2: Russian Federation in Q1 and Q2 2022. From Q3 2022 to Q3 2025, only publicly available online\nL31@P2: data on corridors originating in Russia were collected, i.e., mystery shopping or data collection via\nL32@P2: Overview\nL33@P2: Remittance Prices Worldwide (RPW) monitors remittance prices across all geographic regions of the\nL34@P2: world. Launched in September 2008, RPW monitors the cost incurred by remitters when sending money\nL35@P2: along major remittance corridors. RPW is used as a reference for measuring progress towards global\nL36@P2: cost reduction objectives, including the G20 commitment to reduce the global average to 3 percent,\nL37@P2: which is being pursued in partnership with governments, service providers, and other stakeholders.\nL38@P2: RPW covers 48 remittance sending countries and 102 receiving countries, for a total of 358 country\nL39@P2: corridors worldwide. RPW tracks the cost of sending remittances for four main RSP types: Banks, MTOs,\nL40@P2: Mobile Operators, and Post Offices. MTOs include both traditional providers and innovative/fintech\nL41@P2: players. On average, 19 services per corridor are tracked.\nL42@P2: This Report uses data from RPW’s most recent release to analyze the global, regional, and country\nL43@P2: specific trends in the average cost of migrant remittances.\nL44@P2: FXC Intelligence provides the underlying data used in the RPW.\nL45@P2: Overview\nL46@P2: Remittance Prices Worldwide (RPW) monitors remittance prices across all geographic regions of the\nL47@P2: world. Launched in September 2008, RPW monitors the cost incurred by remitters when sending money\nL48@P2: along major remittance corridors. RPW is used as a reference for measuring progress towards global\nL49@P2: cost reduction objectives, including the G20 commitment to reduce the global average to 5 percent,\nL50@P2: which is being pursued in partnership with governments, service providers, and other stakeholders.\nL51@P2: Since Q2 2016, RPW covers 48 remittance sending countries and 105 receiving countries, for a total of\nL52@P2: 367 country corridors worldwide. RPW tracks the cost of sending remittances for four main RSP types:\nL53@P2: Banks, MTOs, Mobile Operators, and Post Offices. MTOs include both traditional providers and\nL54@P2: innovative/fintech players. On average, 21.7 services per corridor are tracked.\nL55@P2: This Report uses data from RPW’s most recent release to analyze the global, regional, and country\nL56@P2: specific trends in the average cost of migrant remittances.\nL57@P2: FXC Intelligence provides the underlying data used in the RPW.\nL58@P2: Overview\nL59@P2: Remittance Prices Worldwide (RPW) monitors remittance prices across all geographic regions of the\nL60@P2: world. Launched in September 2008, RPW monitors the cost incurred by remitters when sending money\nL61@P2: along major remittance corridors. RPW is used as a reference for measuring progress towards global\nL62@P2: cost reduction objectives, including the G20 commitment to reduce the global average to 5 percent,\nL63@P2: which is being pursued in partnership with governments, service providers, and other stakeholders.\nL64@P2: Since Q2 2016, RPW covers 48 remittance sending countries and 105 receiving countries, for a total of\nL65@P2: 367 country corridors worldwide. RPW tracks the cost of sending remittances for four main RSP types:\nL66@P2: Banks, MTOs, Mobile Operators, and Post Offices. MTOs include both traditional providers and\nL67@P2: innovative/fintech players. On average, 21.7 services per corridor are tracked.\nL68@P2-3: This Report uses data from RPW’s most recent release to analyze the global, regional, and country Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL69@P3: 4\nL70@P3: Official Use Only\nL71@P3: APIs were not employed. Due to this deviation in data collection methodology, these services were\nL72@P3: not included in the main analysis in Q3 2025; instead, the data are presented in Annex V.\nL73@P3: • In Q3 2025, five hundred and nineteen services were moved from the prospects index to the main\nL74@P3: analysis, after demonstrating consistency, materiality and diversity. In the same quarter, 138 new\nL75@P3: services were added to the prospects index. RPW will continue to monitor and include new services\nL76@P3-4: when and where relevant using the prospects index.Remittance Prices Worldwide - Issue 54, September 2025\nL77@P4: 5\nL78@P4: Official Use Only\nL79@P4: Table of Contents\nL80@P4: Overview...........................................................................................................................3\nL81@P4: Key findings ......................................................................................................................3\nL82@P4: Progress tracker ...............................................................................................................6\nL83@P4: Prospects index ................................................................................................................6\nL84@P4: Global trends ....................................................................................................................6\nL85@P4: Global average experiences a decrease ...............................................................................................................6\nL86@P4: International MTO index.....................................................................................................................................8\nL87@P4: Global weighted average.....................................................................................................................................8\nL88@P4: Smart Remitter Target (SmaRT).........................................................................................................................9\nL89@P4: Trends in corridor average 